  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nLow pressure deepens over IA today, then lifts north into MN and \nrestrengthens on Wednesday. This keeps the local area under \npersistent wrap around warm advection. With plenty of moisture, \noccasional rain is expected. The better chance for rain will be east \nof highway 81 this afternoon, then east of the James River on \nWednesday as lighter rainfall spreads westward as the low deepens \nover MN. Low pressure finally begins to drift east Wednesday \nafternoon into Wednesday night which will bring an end to the \nrainfall. Additional rainfall amounts will likely be a half an inch \nto an inch over the next two days. Areas west of the James will see \nthe least amount of rain.\n\nThursday should see marginally breezy northwest flow help dry out \nand scour out most of the cloud cover. However the low level \nthermal field is still on the cool side, so highs will generally be \nfrom 45 to 52 degrees.\n\nA nose of surface high pressure builds into the area Friday morning \nbringing temperatures down into the upper 20s to lower 30s in most \nlocations. Will need to keep an eye on the potential for fog given \nthe very moist soil conditions. If fog can develop with sub-freezing \ntemperatures, roads could end up slippery in the morning.\n\nSunday through Tuesday will see a stronger northern stream jet bring \nsome milder temperatures with very low chances for rainfall. Lows \nwill likely be from the mid 30s to lower 40s with highs in the mid \n50s to lower 60s.",
